#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198007 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38bfb48e-9b66-58a8-966a-fb6938e08073" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation That Determines a Minimum Distance"}] :question/id #uuid "39ffb646-57fa-5d6d-a81f-286948f66d83" :question/math-academy-id "e-12596" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/eec7d2b67f0b76bb8ec6874cf63f8cb09005f451d684924118983ea1aa39269c.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation \n\n$$\n2x + \\bbox[3pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAA^2}}=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x, y)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right).$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{x}))}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡(\\frac{1}{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{x})}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{2}{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{x}) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{2}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =0 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\dfrac{2\\ln x}{x}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198008 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbd4e2eb-d2c8-5447-9a28-4d7da6744712" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Closest Points on a Curve to a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "4b55bd03-c8ad-55b2-a51e-7f875d382627" :question/math-academy-id "e-12597" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points on the curve $y=x^2$ that are closest to the point $\\left( 0, \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $\\left( 0, \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)$ and a general point $P(x, y)$:\n\n$$\nS = x^2 + \\left(y- \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=x^2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nS(x) &=  x^2 + \\left(y- \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2  \\[5pt]\n&=  x^2 + \\left( x^2 - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 \\[5pt]\n&=  x^2 +  x^4 -3x^2 + \\dfrac{9}{4}\\[5pt]\n&=  x^4 - 2 x^2 + \\dfrac{9}{4}.\\\n}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=4{x}^{3}-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are multiple stationary points, we need to use the candidates test to identify the points of minimum distance. We compute the squared distances for each of the stationary points as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(0) & =(0)^{4}-2(0)^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ S(-1) & =(-1)^{4}-2(-1)^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ S(1) & =(1)^{4}-2(1)^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=\\pm 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198011 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a7dfacc-de7b-555f-b83a-7b2c6faf69df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Smallest Distance From a Point to a Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "2bf999cc-9733-5f29-90e6-20d297efe352" :question/math-academy-id "e-12606" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/44ef8fc83ea0a70dcc16769ee6965727ec2c9584e97cc417e34184d633bbf876.png)\n\n  \n\nWhat is the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x^2+3}+1$ and the point $(3,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,1)$ and a general point $P(x, y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x^2+3}+1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+3}+1-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+3})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+2{x}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-6x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=6x-6 & =0 \\\\ 6x & =6 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that $S''(1) =6 > 0.$ Therefore, $x = 1$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{S(1)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3(1)^{2}-6(1)+12} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356891 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38bfb48e-9b66-58a8-966a-fb6938e08073" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation That Determines a Minimum Distance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7749ee96-a62d-556b-bbdc-70fd94edfaa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356893} {:answer/id #uuid "d5907140-1688-57f7-ac12-799aa6a208f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)}{x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356894} {:answer/id #uuid "d4bc8604-6712-5d7e-b19f-8be9913c9103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)}{2x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356895} {:answer/id #uuid "709d1deb-a452-5e50-af70-36c13ca7d3c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356896} {:answer/id #uuid "a243c93e-d258-5f2e-b8ab-62e5253595e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5907140-1688-57f7-ac12-799aa6a208f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)}{x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356894} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff6533f3-5eaf-5a0f-badf-b12124cb326b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f0d654b-3e49-533d-b1f9-de2c0231e2e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-118387" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/3ae609102d5545d3db2e035238f030e3ee1b58be8aba716224d5df967ca6ed5a.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation \n\n$$\n2x+=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2).$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2))^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2} & =0 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)}{x-2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38bfb48e-9b66-58a8-966a-fb6938e08073" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation That Determines a Minimum Distance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "904f4034-f46d-54e4-8eeb-e353c591d0e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356900} {:answer/id #uuid "0e2f437e-7399-55cf-ad26-b5fe4875ab88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356901} {:answer/id #uuid "bebde464-721f-53b0-a0c3-f508baa5c5bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356902} {:answer/id #uuid "65b7bcd8-4e58-5836-b419-0b60a7ffd348" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356903} {:answer/id #uuid "6dc5db0f-6ce4-570a-93e6-cbfc10cf7e4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e2f437e-7399-55cf-ad26-b5fe4875ab88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356901} :answer-field/id #uuid "562e643b-aa2e-58a2-ab6f-f4a00594d324" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0024a60-f6e5-5b56-9272-60dda6ebe058" :question/math-academy-id "q-118389" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/37434a61262f829160fc2b19fcadb67745a024d589cd1ce318ca4ab90d6ab2d9.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y=\\frac{x+1}{\\sqrt{x}}$ that is closest to the point $(2,0)$ satisfies the equation\n\n$$\n2x-3-=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\frac{x+1}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{(\\frac{x+1}{\\sqrt{x}})}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+1}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+x+2+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3x+\\frac{1}{x}+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356905 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbd4e2eb-d2c8-5447-9a28-4d7da6744712" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Closest Points on a Curve to a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b74aaa8f-819f-5a1f-a71c-d69d04f81624" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356907} {:answer/id #uuid "1b448c40-7f1a-5e38-950a-49c4e5d5a308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356908} {:answer/id #uuid "3932a8f0-0650-5572-ae52-4a7ad5eec420" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356909} {:answer/id #uuid "cab2a31f-3791-5493-9aba-868a4472b15e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356910} {:answer/id #uuid "194a4bd9-cf24-5f65-a674-eeb14123a0f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cab2a31f-3791-5493-9aba-868a4472b15e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356910} :answer-field/id #uuid "929d8bae-ac9f-50c1-9136-72d18a543c9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd6ca452-288c-5ce2-9e2c-cfd5a0b16faf" :question/math-academy-id "q-17073" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/f6abba797edf9bd83882c31c2c45729fdcdb40226bf95cc10f0f01c90c68b5f1.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+2}$ that is closest to the point $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-2)^{2}+(y-0)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+(\\sqrt{x+2})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+x+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+x+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3x+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-3 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $D(x)$ are the points where the derivative ${D}^{′}(x)$ becomes $0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2x-3 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356912 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbd4e2eb-d2c8-5447-9a28-4d7da6744712" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Closest Points on a Curve to a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "916b08b3-54c9-5432-bc34-27abe250a93f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356914} {:answer/id #uuid "0b096aa6-4590-533e-a53b-cbf0e522152c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356915} {:answer/id #uuid "94344d14-dc6f-59f8-8852-3aa0fbf49e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356916} {:answer/id #uuid "0504c0f6-0eec-5a92-ac73-30ecb52943ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356917} {:answer/id #uuid "8bc71077-becd-5867-8e56-95ee44a7ad20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94344d14-dc6f-59f8-8852-3aa0fbf49e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356916} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea8581f2-61f5-5cb7-b25d-7b786f325d4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "278753be-fb59-51f2-9074-3b38572b1673" :question/math-academy-id "q-211191" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/4e343d2884532c006e83cd4c90e70a6cd9af9247194c8a7f8832ef8cbaf293b5.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2}$ that is closest to the point $(4,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(4,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-4)^{2}+(y)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-4)^{2}+(y)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2})}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+{x}^{2}+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+16+{x}^{2}+2 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-8x+18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=4x-8 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =8 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=4>0.$ Therefore, $x=2$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbd4e2eb-d2c8-5447-9a28-4d7da6744712" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Closest Points on a Curve to a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fdc6ed2-b96b-5b07-9151-04686e2b6249" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356921} {:answer/id #uuid "02cc79e7-6e92-52d6-a9eb-491e7a746fd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356922} {:answer/id #uuid "1d353a4b-df85-56ef-999d-d09a6e6004c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356923} {:answer/id #uuid "d776178c-8b52-5b93-89ce-2a1ed092a35a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356924} {:answer/id #uuid "9102b27a-fefe-5981-856e-33e3bce2d666" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02cc79e7-6e92-52d6-a9eb-491e7a746fd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356922} :answer-field/id #uuid "f97d846c-c70a-5197-b005-cb540564dc80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ebe8b79a-ce45-501c-ba46-43ab7537ad37" :question/math-academy-id "q-211194" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-2}+2$ that is closest to the point $(3,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,2)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-2}+2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{x-2}+2-2)}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+x-2 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-5x+7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-5 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(\\frac{5}{2})=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a7dfacc-de7b-555f-b83a-7b2c6faf69df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Smallest Distance From a Point to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2e48e1c-028c-5f28-98d4-67f9c2a68c03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356928} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee2da7b-8b84-5e2b-8512-9827af26c3ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356929} {:answer/id #uuid "5138639e-225f-5812-b39a-bbfd59b6b12d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356930} {:answer/id #uuid "99b0257c-8458-5311-95ac-2d6a4eff47f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356931} {:answer/id #uuid "cf3aad28-5701-59b1-96e9-814a38674d51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99b0257c-8458-5311-95ac-2d6a4eff47f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356931} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d925153-91ae-5078-8c6e-4a25bdd237a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "701c426f-a0c3-5762-b57a-ba562a9cb695" :question/math-academy-id "q-211282" :question/problem "What is the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x}+2$ and the point $P(5,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(5,2)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-5)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x}+2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-5)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x}+2-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-10x+25+2x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-8 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =8 \\\\ x & =\\frac{8}{2} \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=4$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=4$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(4)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(4)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}-8(4)+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16-32+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a7dfacc-de7b-555f-b83a-7b2c6faf69df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Smallest Distance From a Point to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f68f8b4-85ed-5c0a-af89-c579d97962e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f68f8b4-85ed-5c0a-af89-c579d97962e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356935} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffbbc6de-b10b-5f30-b24e-2c9a843b9a06" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c87b802d-11f7-5092-8650-55e1b7a4acef" :question/math-academy-id "q-315645" :question/problem "Find the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{4x-1}+2$ and the point $P(4,2).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(4,2)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{4x-1}+2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{4x-1}+2-2)}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+(\\sqrt{4x-1})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+4x-1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+16+4x-1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-4 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=2$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(2)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}-4\\,(2)+15} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4-8+15} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbd4e2eb-d2c8-5447-9a28-4d7da6744712" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Closest Points on a Curve to a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a0dcabf-f85d-507e-ab17-0841cc595107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a0dcabf-f85d-507e-ab17-0841cc595107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356938} :answer-field/id #uuid "daa8ae59-e10e-5893-a65d-296ad4f59f95" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70f7604d-a827-588c-b0d6-d2226b6a5f91" :question/math-academy-id "q-344820" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=\\sqrt{4x+5}+2$ that is closest to the point $(3,2).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,2)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{4x+5}+2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{4x+5}+2-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+4x+5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+4x+5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-2 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =2 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=1$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356939 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbd4e2eb-d2c8-5447-9a28-4d7da6744712" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Closest Points on a Curve to a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38a99c1b-04cd-5a81-84a1-f33e3af185c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38a99c1b-04cd-5a81-84a1-f33e3af185c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356941} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee543076-7ce5-536d-9e9f-e6e9c432629e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d139748-7409-5b25-b840-b55a718e3b8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-344831" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/be3108802cde61c1ffcc5fdf603e97255b4082541f20f628aedd1a01127c05b7.png)\n\nFind the $y$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+6x$ that is closest to the point $(3,0).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+{y}^{2}+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+6x.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & =-y+{y}^{2}+9 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}-y+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(y)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y)=2y-1 & =0 \\\\ 2y & =1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(y)=2>0.$ Therefore, $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a7dfacc-de7b-555f-b83a-7b2c6faf69df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Smallest Distance From a Point to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f6c161a-b194-5006-a476-6011e426dc77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f6c161a-b194-5006-a476-6011e426dc77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356944} :answer-field/id #uuid "a28dda0a-005b-59bd-a8d5-bb62b3e91c61" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a185d3d-1d4f-52d3-ba5d-86f674f6bdc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-344839" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/4108c44d3fac12cc38c0aab73ed1600f034ad88c1c50be91ea7bb29145ce4dd7.png)\n\nFind the least distance $d$ between the curve $y=\\sqrt{|x-1|}+3$ and the point $(3,3).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,3)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-3)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{|x-1|}+3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{|x-1|}+3-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{|x-1|})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+|x-1|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ge 1,$ we have $|x-1|=x-1,$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-3)^{2}+(x-1) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+x-1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-5x+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-5 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0,$ so $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ gives a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{5}{2})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{5}{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-5(\\frac{5}{2})+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a7dfacc-de7b-555f-b83a-7b2c6faf69df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Smallest Distance From a Point to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3d7f20f-a379-5e90-8bb6-8ed8178233e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356947} {:answer/id #uuid "3711f8a8-b24b-5809-9b9d-552f164a5544" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3d7f20f-a379-5e90-8bb6-8ed8178233e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356947} :answer-field/id #uuid "573eb4f6-5e0c-53c7-b5ee-da0ba26bb767" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c3a7702-c807-5572-8902-3d9c8c59af55" :question/math-academy-id "q-344845" :question/problem "Find the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x+2}+1$ and the point $P(3,1).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,1)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x+2}+1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2x+2}+1-1)}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x+2})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+2x+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+2x+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-4 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=2$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(2)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}-4(2)+11} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4-8+11} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a7dfacc-de7b-555f-b83a-7b2c6faf69df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Smallest Distance From a Point to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1174c62-c830-5bd5-9187-2dd933ea8280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1174c62-c830-5bd5-9187-2dd933ea8280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356951} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdb10735-f330-546c-8f21-b9c5ed2e23e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "117a1e44-2d63-563a-83d8-0eda966066d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-344846" :question/problem "Find the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}+4}+2$ and the point $P(1,2).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(1,2)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}+4}+2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}+4}+2-2)}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}+4})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+(x-1)^{2}+4 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-4x+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=4x-4 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =4 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=4>0.$ Therefore, $x=1$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(1)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2(1)^{2}-4(1)+6} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2-4+6} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356952 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38bfb48e-9b66-58a8-966a-fb6938e08073" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation That Determines a Minimum Distance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f826d25a-f4cd-561a-b5f1-d32be041fda2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356954} {:answer/id #uuid "d72b904e-f89f-5359-a3f8-5ef7fded1f3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356955} {:answer/id #uuid "2e4153d6-0d91-5d40-b56e-23bd30886145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356956} {:answer/id #uuid "a0522629-61e6-5836-80ed-8cad472fc93a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356957} {:answer/id #uuid "e22f5755-4d27-5467-9304-d37e81ac749e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f826d25a-f4cd-561a-b5f1-d32be041fda2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356954} :answer-field/id #uuid "e47ad111-ae8e-5b7d-bcab-a38dba14e569" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc95ebdf-6548-58a8-a119-21651462d0b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-49049" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/c924791712d00c951d9718fa976a00f35b2b3deb35d83301431ddb55d22326de.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y={e}^{2x}$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation \n\n$$\n2x+=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={e}^{2x}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+({e}^{2x})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+4{e}^{4x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356959 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38bfb48e-9b66-58a8-966a-fb6938e08073" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation That Determines a Minimum Distance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ca22505-7493-5625-b2e5-91f2d3ea229d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356961} {:answer/id #uuid "d29aa882-ffe2-5c8e-9af5-3eba09f685a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356962} {:answer/id #uuid "f67884e0-d673-5a66-a3e7-4b29bb24482a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356963} {:answer/id #uuid "60aae3cc-7c90-5068-865b-912ffecdc4f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡x}{x{log}_{2}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356964} {:answer/id #uuid "58fc8055-430a-5bdf-b935-ab43ced09a5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{log}_{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ca22505-7493-5625-b2e5-91f2d3ea229d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356961} :answer-field/id #uuid "2266363b-fa3c-5ec3-818f-fdb3cd80e4c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "efe15852-9d1f-5666-a4ba-bce491f20576" :question/math-academy-id "q-49051" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/22505181ad0a10e74a158edabc949444055f1deda57286ff63674c32f2dc79c7.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y={log}_{2}⁡x$  that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation \n\n$$\n2x+=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={log}_{2}⁡x.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{log}_{2}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+2{log}_{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} & =0 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38bfb48e-9b66-58a8-966a-fb6938e08073" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation That Determines a Minimum Distance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "583ffb9f-498c-54dc-a34f-d19284542a69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356968} {:answer/id #uuid "69bb8274-2305-551d-a829-9ff734840f81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}{sin}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356969} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e3061e-d203-5367-b3d3-22ada90d2ca5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356970} {:answer/id #uuid "1fd4683a-e5de-5119-97e6-c4d28a059f1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356971} {:answer/id #uuid "5c9dead5-9409-537c-adf0-2dbebb96e15a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6e3061e-d203-5367-b3d3-22ada90d2ca5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356970} :answer-field/id #uuid "910eb097-3301-5235-8eb8-a119cbfe5d0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb0ef12c-9b61-582b-acd5-7493f7def056" :question/math-academy-id "q-49054" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/47849c87c3c8cf9008b6531e740c6fc57058905cf17e31e4c93c479f337f0fbd.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y={e}^{x}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ that is closest to the point $(1,0)$ satisfies the equation\n\n$$\n2x-2+=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(1,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={e}^{x}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{({e}^{x}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-2+{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ 2x-2+{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
